monitor: retry a rejected DPMS commit instead of latching a false state

CMonitor::setDPMS assigns m_dpmsStatus before the commit meant to realise it,
and returns early whenever that cached value already matches the request.
commitDPMSState retries once, two frames later, and gives up silently if the
retry fails too. On a 240Hz output those two frames are 8ms.

A single rejected enable therefore leaves the compositor reporting a monitor
that is on while the panel stays dark, and every later "dpms on" is discarded by
the early return before it can reach the hardware. The only way out is toggling
to off and back, which is what users end up doing repeatedly. Nothing on the
sink side helps -- power cycling the display, reseating the cable and switching
its input all fail -- because the source never attempts the commit again.

Split the two meanings apart. m_dpmsStatus now records what committed, and
remains what hyprctl, wlr-output-power, the session lock and the Lua bindings
read. m_dpmsTarget records what was asked for. The two differ only while a
request is in flight or after one was rejected, and setDPMS treats that
disagreement as work still outstanding rather than as a redundant request.

The toggle action follows the target rather than what committed. Toggling states
an intent -- the opposite of what was last asked for -- and deriving it from the
committed state means a rejected disable leaves every following press asking to
disable again, each discarded as redundant while the retry is still outstanding.
The presses do nothing until the retries run out, which is the same dance from
the other side: the user is pressing a key that cannot reach the hardware. Only
the toggle changes; dpms on and dpms off state their direction outright and
never consulted the cached value.

Retries now back off from 50ms to a 2s ceiling across eight attempts, roughly
seven seconds, rather than taking one shot 8ms later. That is long enough to
outlast the conditions which cause the rejection: a disable and an enable
arriving almost together, or a connector still settling after a hotplug. When
the attempts are exhausted the target is reconciled with what is actually live,
so intent and reality agree again and the next toggle asks for the opposite of
what is on the panel.

The retry timer is cancelled and removed from the event loop before being
replaced, and on monitor disconnect, matching clearModeRetry() in the same file.
Previously a failed retry left it registered for the lifetime of the monitor.
